398:
(a) Any person, firm, or corporation desiring to operate a vape shop in the City of Byron shall submit an application for a license to the city administrator or his/her designee.
(b) The application shall include, but not be limited to, the following information:
(1) Name, age, and residence address of the owner-applicant;
(2) If the owner-applicant is a corporate entity, the names and addresses of the officers or members, the name and address of the registered agent, the state, county, and date of incorporation or formation, and the complete names and addresses of the administrator or administrators of the proposed vape shop;
(3) The address of the location where the vape shop is to be located and operated;
(4) Any previous experience the administrator and owner(s) have had in the operation of vape shops or similar businesses;
(5) Any vape shop license history of the owner-applicant and the administrator, including whether such person in any previous operation in any jurisdiction has had such a license revoked or suspended, the reason therefor, and any business activity or occupation subsequent to the action of suspension or revocation;
(6) The name and address of the owner of the property where the vape shop is to be located; and
(7) A complete set of fingerprints of the applicant, the administrators), and the owner(s).
(c) The city administrator or his/her designee shall have 30 calendar days after the application is completed and filed to investigate the applicant and issue or deny the license.
(Ord. No. 2024-7, 9-9-2024)

400:
A vape shop shall:
(1) Prohibit gambling or other games of chance; the prohibition in this provision includes, but is not limited to, coin operated amusement machines (COAMs) as that term is defined by Georgia statutes and by section 10-31 of article 2 of chapter 10 of this Code;
(2) During the time when the establishment is open for business, prohibit entry thereto by a person under the age of 21, and post signs at each entrance and exit clearly stating that a person under the age of 21 is prohibited from entry, regardless of whether the vape shop is or is not an accessory use to a nightclub or other use;
(3) Prohibit the sale of malt beverages, wines, distilled spirits, and other alcoholic beverages;
(4) Prohibit the use of or consumption of any vape product or device within the vape shop under all circumstances, including, but not limited to, testing, tasting, choosing, or for any other reason;
(5) During the hours of operation, the interior of the premises of a vape shop shall be maintained with adequate illumination to make the conduct of patrons within the premise readily discernible to a person with normal vision.
(Ord. No. 2025-12, 8-11-2025)

405:
A hookah bar, shop, or lounge shall:
(1) Prohibit gambling or other games of chance; the prohibition in this provision includes, but is not limited to, coin operated amusement machines (COAMs) as that term is defined by Georgia statutes and by section 10-31 of article 2 of chapter 10 of this Code;
(2) During the time when the establishment is open for business, prohibit entry thereto by a person under the age of 21, and post signs at each entrance and exit clearly stating that a person under the age of 21 is prohibited from entry, regardless of whether the hookah establishment is or is not an accessory use to a nightclub or other use;
(3) Prohibit the sale of malt beverages, wines, distilled spirits, or other alcoholic beverages;
(4) Abide by the following air ventilation requirements: hookah establishment must have and maintain a ventilation system that exhausts smoke from the business and is designed in accordance with the state building code standards for the occupancy classification in use. The air handling systems from the smoking area shall be independent from the main air handling system that serves all other areas of the building, and all air within the smoking area shall be exhausted directly to the outside by an exhaust fan. No air from the smoking area shall be recirculated to other parts of the building.
(5) During the hours of operation, the interior of the premises of a hookah establishment shall be maintained with adequate illumination to make the conduct of patrons within the premise readily discernible to a person with normal vision.
(Ord. No. 2025-12, 8-11-2025)

406:
(a) Required. All persons, firms, companies, partnerships, corporations, or other such entities desiring to operate a hookah shop, bar, or lounge shall, prior to commencing such business, comply with all rules and regulations adopted by the governing authority regulating the operation of hookah shops, bars, or lounges. It shall be unlawful for any person, firm, or corporation to operate a hookah shop, bar, or lounge without having first obtained a license. All licenses shall expire on December 31 of the calendar year in which it was granted, and reapplication shall be required for each year thereafter.
(b) Application. The application shall include, but shall not be limited to, the information required on all occupational tax returns, along with the following:
(1) Name, age, and residence address of the owner-applicant;
(2) If the owner-applicant is a corporate entity, the names and addresses of the officers or members; the name and address of the registered agent and the registered office of the corporate entity; the state, county, and date of incorporation or formation; and the complete names and addresses of the administrator or administrators of the proposed hookah shop, bar, or lounge;
(3) The name of all shareholders holding 20 percent or more of any class of corporate stock;
(4) The address of the location where the hookah shop, bar, or lounge is to be located and operated;
(5) All the previous experience the administrator and owner(s) have had in operation of hookah shops, bars, or lounges;
(6) Any hookah shop, bar, or lounge license history of the owner-applicant and the administrator, including whether such person, in any previous operation in any jurisdiction, has had such a license revoked or suspended, the reason therefor, and any business activity or occupation subsequent to the action of suspension or revocation;
(7) The name and address of the owner of the property where the hookah shop, bar, or lounge is to be located;
(8) A complete set of fingerprints of the applicant, the administrator(s), and the owner(s) (hereinafter collectively referred to as "applicant"):
a. Authority. This subsection is enacted pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 35-3-35(a)(1.2), the Georgia Umbrella Statute entitled Dissemination of Information to Public Agencies, Political Subdivisions, Authorities, and Instrumentalities;
b. Fingerprinting required. An individual seeking to receive a hookah shop, bar, or lounge license shall be fingerprinted as a condition of submitting an application for said license. By filing such application, the applicant consents to the city obtaining their criminal history record information (CHRI) from the Georgia Crime Information Center (GCIC) and the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI);
c. Access authorized; usage regulations.
i. The city is hereby authorized to access national criminal history record information pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 35-3-35(a)(1.2).
ii. Information obtained and handled for any purposes pursuant to this subsection shall comply with all state laws and the Federal Privacy Act.
iii. No information given as part of the request for a criminal history and no record obtained pursuant to this section may be entered on any database. No information given or obtained pursuant to this section shall be subject to the provisions of the Georgia Open Records Act.
d. Administration; fees for Byron Police Department; procedures.
i. Applicant shall provide his/her fingerprints, which may be taken by the city police department or may be taken by the Peach County Sheriff's Office if the city police department is unable to do so.
ii. The applicant shall bear the cost of:
1. Fingerprinting if carried out by the Peach County Sheriff;
2. The charge by the GBI and the FBI for the CHRI history at their latest rate, paid to the city by certified or cashier's check or money order; and
3. An administrative fee allowed by statute to be charged by the city, which shall be the maximum allowed by statute, which is currently $20.00, which fee shall include fingerprinting when carried out by the city police department;
iii. Upon receipt of the fingerprints and the appropriate fees, the city will transmit said fingerprints and appropriate fees (unless a satisfactory billing arrangement has been entered into between the city police department and the GCIC and/or FBI) to the GCIC. As provided by law, the GCIC will compare the subject's fingerprints against its criminal file and submit the fingerprints to the FBI for a comparison with nation-wide records, unless submission to the FBI is automatic pursuant to the use of live-scan. The results of the FBI check will be returned based on its current procedure, presently being directly to the city police department if submissions are made manually to the GCIC, or electronically from both the GBI and FBI where submissions are made electronically to the GCIC.
iv. In compliance with Federal Law 95-544, which provides for the rendering of a fitness determination, the city will decide whether the record applicant has been convicted of, or is under pending indictment for enumerated disqualifiers, as set forth within this chapter.
v. A person who has consented to the city for a criminal history based on fingerprinting record may request and receive a copy of the criminal history record report from the city at no additional charge. Should the person seek to amend or correct the record, he or she shall be responsible for contacting the GCIC as to state records and/or the FBI concerning records from other jurisdictions maintained in its file.
(9) Failure to furnish required information, and in the form designated, shall automatically serve to dismiss the application;
(10) All applications shall be signed and sworn to by the applicant before a notary public or other officer authorized to administer oaths;
(11) In addition to the application fee due to the police department, the applicant shall also pay a $50.00 application fee to the planning and inspections department to process the application;
(12) The city shall have 14 calendar days after the application is completed and filed with the city administrator or his/her designee in which to investigate the applicant and issue or deny the license. No license to operate a hookah shop, bar, or lounge will be issued if the applicant:
a. Has been convicted of, or has entered a plea of guilty or nolo contendere to, a misdemeanor involving moral turpitude, illegal gambling, any felony, criminal trespass, public indecency, misdemeanor involving any type of sexual-related crime, any theft or violence against person or property, any crime of possession, sale, or distribution of illegal drugs, distribution of material depicting nudity or sexual conduct as defined under state law, criminal solicitation to commit any of these listed offenses, attempts to commit any of these listed offenses, for a period of five years prior to the date of application for such certificate and has been released from parole or probation. As used herein, the term "a misdemeanor involving moral turpitude" shall include any misdemeanor criminal offenses involving fraud, theft, dishonesty, and misdemeanor drug offenses. However, all applicants shall be entitled to the full benefits of O.C.G.A. § 42-8-60 et seq. relating to first offender status. If, at the time of application, the applicant is charged with any of the offenses prescribed in this subsection, then consideration of the application shall be suspended until the entry of a plea or verdict or dismissal;
b. Has had any previous license to operate a hookah shop, bar, or lounge revoked; or
c. Has violated any other hookah shop, bar, or lounge regulation.
(13) In any case in which it appears to the city administrator or his/her designee that an applicant is not entitled to the issuance of a license under the provisions of this chapter, the city administrator or his/her designee shall so notify the applicant in writing by mailing said notice to the last address furnished by the applicant. In the event that the city administrator or his/her designee refuses to issue a hookah shop, bar, or lounge license, or in the event that a hookah shop, bar, or lounge license is surrendered pursuant to the provisions of this chapter, the applicant or license holder shall have an absolute right of appeal to the city administrator according to the procedures set forth herein. The appeal shall be perfected by filing with the city clerk a notice of appeal. The notice of appeal must be filed with the city clerk with a copy to the city administrator, within 14 days following the mailing of the notification of denial or surrender of the license. Thereafter, the city administrator shall set a date for hearing on the matter within 30 days after the city administrator's receipt of the notice of appeal. Moreover, the city administrator shall notify the appellant in writing of the date, time, and place when the matter shall be heard. After the hearing, the city administrator shall inform the applicant of the decision within 14 calendar days of the hearing. Should the applicant/appellant wish to appeal the decision of the city administrator, the applicant must file an appeal within five days after the date of the administrator's decision to the mayor and city council. The mayor and city council may review the appeal of the administrator's decision at a specially called meeting, a work session, or at its next regularly scheduled meeting and may reverse, affirm, or modify in any regard the determination of the city administrator. All appeals shall be filed with the city clerk who shall provide copies of the appeal to the city administrator, designee, mayor, and council. The decision of the mayor and city council shall be final.
(Ord. No. 2024-7, 9-9-2024; Ord. No. 2025-9, 5-12-2025; Ord. No. 2025-12, 8-11-2025)
